{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791620168,"nonce":"iwFf4ddZ90lD/4LLuNzctwDOa4o=","message":"Items 1 and 2 landed in 52eb109d5c0b19fe021f3e99e5b844ab03484599. Verified by rendering a new notch_joins scene (concave top, bulge top, peak top, bulge bottom with concave bottom corners, 2px border) before and after: 160 pixels change out of 480000, all at the joins (flare ends, the peak's base, the bottom flares), the bulge and the fills are unchanged, and the tick at the flare ends is gone. The rest of the visual suite is identical with and without the change. tests/notch_joins.rs pins the joins and that the notch shaders validate; both mutations (flares blended again, bulge back to the join depth) fail it. The vb and dt variants were not run on a GPU here.\n\nOne difference from the reporter's formula: the bulge's width clip applies only below the base line (min of the strip and the half-plane), so a tall bulge whose circle is wider than the cap above the base keeps its shape. For a shallow bulge, and for the peak, it is the same as clipping to the width.\n\nItem 3 does not apply to Blinc: the Notch element has a drop shadow only (fill_notch takes one shadow, no inset), and no inner_shadow reaches a notched node, so there is no inset ring to follow the notch. Giving Notch inset shadows would be a feature, not a fix.","files":null}]}